        "id": 11002,
        "name": "Specific heat capacity",
        "slug": "aphy_th_02",
        "content": "# Specific Heat Capacity\n\n## Definition\n\nSpecific heat capacity (c) is the energy required to raise the temperature of **1 kg** of a substance by **1 K** (or 1°C).\n\n$$Q = mc\\Delta\\theta$$\n\n| Symbol | Quantity | Unit |\n|--------|----------|------|\n| Q | Energy supplied | J |\n| m | Mass | kg |\n| c | Specific heat capacity | J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ |\n| Δθ | Temperature change | K or °C |\n\n## Key values to know\n\n| Substance | c (J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) |\n|-----------|----------------|\n| Water | ~4200 |\n| Aluminium | ~900 |\n| Iron/Steel | ~450 |\n| Copper | ~385 |\n\nWater's high specific heat capacity makes it an excellent coolant.\n\n## Experimental determination\n\n**Electrical method:**\n- Heat a block of known mass using an immersion heater\n- Measure voltage (V), current (I), time (t) and temperature change (Δθ)\n- Assume all electrical energy → thermal energy: $VIt = mc\\Delta\\theta$\n- Rearrange: $c = \\frac{VIt}{m\\Delta\\theta}$\n\n**Sources of error:** heat loss to surroundings → measured c is higher than true value. Insulate the block and account for heat capacity of the heater.\n\n## Common exam mistakes\n\n- Forgetting to convert g to kg\n- Using Δθ in °C when you need K (they give the same numerical answer for a *difference*, but be careful)\n- Not accounting for the heat capacity of the heater/container\n\n## Summary\n\n- Q = mcΔθ connects energy, mass and temperature change\n- Larger c → harder to heat up (more energy needed per degree)\n- Water has high c → good coolant/thermal buffer\n"
