        "name": "Specific latent heat",
        "slug": "aphy_th_03",
        "content": "# Specific Latent Heat\n\n## Definition\n\nSpecific latent heat (L) is the energy required to change the state of **1 kg** of a substance **without a temperature change**.\n\n$$Q = mL$$\n\n| Symbol | Quantity | Unit |\n|--------|----------|------|\n| Q | Energy | J |\n| m | Mass | kg |\n| L | Specific latent heat | J kg⁻¹ |\n\n## Two types\n\n| Type | Process | Symbol |\n|------|---------|--------|\n| Latent heat of **fusion** | Solid ↔ Liquid (melting/freezing) | $L_f$ |\n| Latent heat of **vaporisation** | Liquid ↔ Gas (boiling/condensing) | $L_v$ |\n\nFor water: $L_f \\approx 3.34 \\times 10^5\\ \\text{J kg}^{-1}$, $L_v \\approx 2.26 \\times 10^6\\ \\text{J kg}^{-1}$\n\nVaporisation requires much more energy than fusion because molecules must overcome all intermolecular forces to escape, not just partially weaken them.\n\n## What is the energy doing?\n\nDuring a change of state, temperature stays **constant** because:\n- Energy goes into **breaking (or forming) intermolecular bonds** (increasing/decreasing PE)\n- Kinetic energy of molecules stays the same → temperature unchanged\n\n## Heating curve interpretation\n\n```\nTemperature\n    ^\n    |          boiling\n    |       ...........\n    |      /           \\\n    | melting           \\\n    |...                 \\\n    +---------------------→ Heat added\n```\nFlat sections = latent heat being absorbed (state change). Sloped sections = temperature rising (sensible heat).\n\n## Summary\n\n- Q = mL for changes of state\n- Temperature stays constant during state changes\n- Vaporisation requires more energy than fusion\n- Energy breaks bonds (increases PE), not molecular speed\n"
