        "id": 11005,
        "name": "Boyle's, Charles's and Pressure Law",
        "slug": "aphy_th_05",
        "content": "# Boyle's, Charles's and the Pressure Law\n\nThe three gas laws each describe what happens when **one variable is changed while another is kept constant** for a fixed mass of gas.\n\n## Boyle's Law — constant temperature\n\n$$p \\propto \\frac{1}{V} \\implies pV = \\text{constant}$$\n\nSo: $p_1 V_1 = p_2 V_2$\n\n- Graph: p vs V → hyperbola (p vs 1/V → straight line through origin)\n\n**Explanation:** Fewer collisions per unit area if volume increases → lower pressure. At constant T, the speed of molecules is unchanged.\n\n## Charles's Law — constant pressure\n\n$$V \\propto T \\implies \\frac{V}{T} = \\text{constant}$$\n\nSo: $\\frac{V_1}{T_1} = \\frac{V_2}{T_2}$ (T in kelvin!)\n\n- Graph: V vs T(K) → straight line through origin\n\n**Explanation:** Higher temperature → faster molecules → greater force per collision → gas expands to restore constant pressure.\n\n## Pressure Law — constant volume\n\n$$p \\propto T \\implies \\frac{p}{T} = \\text{constant}$$\n\nSo: $\\frac{p_1}{T_1} = \\frac{p_2}{T_2}$ (T in kelvin!)\n\n- Graph: p vs T(K) → straight line through origin\n\n**Explanation:** Higher temperature → faster molecules → more frequent and harder collisions with walls → greater pressure.\n\n## Combined gas law\n\nAll three can be combined for a fixed mass of gas:\n\n$$\\frac{p_1 V_1}{T_1} = \\frac{p_2 V_2}{T_2}$$\n\n## Worked example\n\nA gas at 300 K has pressure 100 kPa and volume 2.0 L. It is heated to 450 K at constant volume. Find the new pressure.\n\n$$\\frac{p_1}{T_1} = \\frac{p_2}{T_2} \\implies p_2 = \\frac{100 \\times 450}{300} = 150\\ \\text{kPa}$$\n\n## Summary\n\n| Law | Constant | Relationship |\n|-----|---------|-------------|\n| Boyle's | Temperature | pV = const |\n| Charles's | Pressure | V/T = const |\n| Pressure | Volume | p/T = const |\n\nAlways convert temperature to **kelvin** before using these equations.\n"
