        "name": "The ideal gas equation pV = nRT",
        "slug": "aphy_th_06",
        "content": "# The Ideal Gas Equation: pV = nRT\n\n## The equation\n\n$$pV = nRT$$\n\n| Symbol | Meaning | Unit |\n|--------|---------|------|\n| p | Pressure | Pa (N m⁻²) |\n| V | Volume | m³ |\n| n | Amount of substance | mol |\n| R | Molar gas constant = 8.31 | J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ |\n| T | Temperature | K |\n\n## Alternative form using number of molecules\n\n$$pV = NkT$$\n\nwhere:\n- N = number of molecules\n- k = Boltzmann constant = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹\n\n**Linking the two:** $k = \\frac{R}{N_A}$ where $N_A = 6.02 \\times 10^{23}$ mol⁻¹ (Avogadro's number)\n\nAlso: $N = nN_A$\n\n## The mole\n\nOne mole contains $N_A = 6.02 \\times 10^{23}$ particles.\n\n- Number of moles: $n = \\frac{\\text{mass (g)}}{\\text{molar mass (g mol}^{-1}\\text{)}}$\n- At STP (0°C, 101 kPa): 1 mole of any ideal gas occupies 22.4 dm³\n\n## Unit check — critical!\n\n| Quantity | Must be in |\n|---------|-----------|\n| p | Pa = N m⁻² (not kPa!) |\n| V | m³ (not dm³ or L!) |\n| T | K (not °C!) |\n| n | mol |\n\nConvert: 1 dm³ = 10⁻³ m³; 1 kPa = 10³ Pa; T(K) = θ(°C) + 273\n\n## Worked example\n\nA 5.0 mol sample of ideal gas is held at 400 K in a 0.10 m³ container. Calculate the pressure.\n\n$$p = \\frac{nRT}{V} = \\frac{5.0 \\times 8.31 \\times 400}{0.10} = 166\\,200\\ \\text{Pa} \\approx 166\\ \\text{kPa}$$\n\n## Summary\n\n- pV = nRT (moles) or pV = NkT (molecules)\n- k = R/Nₐ = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹\n- Always use Pa, m³, K\n- An ideal gas is a theoretical model — real gases deviate at high pressure or low temperature\n"
